by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-14T07:52:59Z

Follow-up: the return value is now type-checked rather than compared to `false`.

`false === $return` silently ignored anything else falsy, so `#[Callback(fn ($v) => preg_match('/x/', $v), message: '...')]` never raised a violation, `preg_match()` returns `0`. Accepting any falsy value instead would have been worse: the classic `function ($object, ExecutionContextInterface $context)` callback returns `null`, so setting `message` on one would have raised a violation on every single validation.

So when `message` is set the callback must return a boolean, and anything else throws:

```
The callback targeted by the Callback constraint must return a boolean when the "message" option is set, "int" returned.
```

When `message` is not set nothing is inspected, so existing callbacks that return `0`, a string or nothing at all are unaffected:

```
legacy: returns false, no message      => 0 violations
legacy: returns 0, no message          => 0 violations
legacy: void callback, no message      => 0 violations
legacy: adds violation + false         => 1 violation (own)
new: false WITH message                => 1 violation (auto)
new: true WITH message                 => 0 violations
WITH message, returns int/void/string  => ConstraintDefinitionException
```

Two tests cover it, the docblock and the changelog now state the contract. Validator is green at 5282 tests on both 8.4 and 8.5.

